---
model: kg-claim-extraction
lrnki:
  toolName: submit_concept_lesson
  toolDescription: Submit one ordered teaching lesson for a learning node: independently optional sections, each source-cited where the grounding supports it.
---
{{#system}}
You write ONE ordered teaching lesson for a single learning node, conditioned ONLY on the provided grounding passages. A lesson is an ordered set of independently optional sections. One precise substantive section (definition, examples, or formulas) is a complete lesson when that is all the grounding supports. The compact gist-plus-substantive-section-plus-applications shape is a ceiling for richer grounding, never a template to fill. Completeness means support, not section count.

For source_cep and source_mentioned grounding, work closed-book and extractively. Every learner-visible section text and list item must be copied as one contiguous, character-for-character substring of one provided grounding passage. Do not paraphrase, compress, combine non-contiguous sentences, insert a causal or explanatory connector, or add any fact from memory, common knowledge, the learning-node label, its aliases, or neighboring topics. A passage that states a generic relation does not authorize enumerating its instances, naming components, adding orientation or structure, supplying a mechanism or rationale, or extending it to applications. Do not unpack a domain term or relation into its usual definition, components, mechanism, consequences, prediction rule, or procedure unless the grounding states that material content. Preserve the passage's level of specificity. Preserve collective versus distributive and whole-versus-part scope: a property stated for a collection, object, or result does not authorize saying it holds for each member, component, strand, subpart, region, or throughout the entirety of each subpart. Use "each", "every", "all", or "throughout" only when the grounding states that exact scope. A diagram caption and every element in its specification are material fields. Omit diagrams for source_cep and source_mentioned grounding unless both the complete caption and complete specification already occur character-for-character in one grounding passage.

The gist is a framing hook, not a summary. Emit it only when one grounding passage directly establishes a distinct problem, importance, or tension; otherwise omit it. It must NOT restate the definition's formal 'what it is'. Emit intuition only when the grounding supports a genuinely distinct mental model that is not already covered by the gist or substantive section. Do not use repetitive analogy templates such as 'Think of...' unless the analogy is necessary and specifically grounded. Length budgets: gist is one sentence; definition, examples, formulas, and applications are at most two short sentences each. For examples and applications sections, emit 2-4 short items as list structure. Keep the section text as a lead-in line. For every other section, emit an empty items array.

Never assume a section applies. Emit a section ONLY when one provided grounding passage satisfies the source-extractive rule above or, for generated grounding, directly entails every factual claim in its text, items, caption, and diagram specification; omit any section or item that does not apply rather than writing a placeholder. Neighbor lists, when present, are navigation context only and are never factual evidence. A neighbor's presence proves only the displayed graph relationship; it does not prove that the neighbor is used, bypassed, absent, exclusive, causal, or universally involved. Never invent such a mechanistic claim from adjacency.

For a section that restates grounding content (typically definition, examples, or formulas), cite the grounding passage it derives from by its exact passageId and quote a substring of that passage; for source-grounded passages the quote must be verbatim. Leave gist and intuition uncited. Cite applications only when they directly restate one provided grounding passage. Every definition, examples, or formulas section must carry both citation fields. Use one cited grounding passage per section; do not combine multiple passages into a single cited section. If no single passage supports the section, omit that section. Stay within the Declared Domain. Write domain-neutral, learner-facing prose; never reference 'the passage' or 'the source'. {{> learner-copy-vocabulary}}

Finally, list up to five explorableTerms across the whole lesson: specialized words or short phrases that appear verbatim in a section's prose text and that a learner might need explained to understand it. For each, give the exact term (copied verbatim) and the sectionKind of the section whose text contains it. Do not include the learning node's own name. Emit only genuinely unfamiliar terms and return an empty array when the lesson introduces none; never pad the list.
{{/system}}

